// ACCT question bank. Written from the LMS Module 1-5 Ebooks and Study Guides only.
// Row: [module, question, options, correctIndex (0-based), reason, source, wrong-option notes]
window.QBANK = {
  subject: "Principles of Accounting", code: "ACCT",
  modules: {"1":"Introduction to Accounting","2":"Primary Book (Journal)","3":"Subsidiary Books","4":"Trial Balance","5":"Final Accounts"},
  rows: [
[1,"Accounting is defined as the art of recording, classifying, summarising financial transactions in monetary terms and interpreting the results. What is the primary output of accounting?",["Tax returns and government reports","Financial Statements (Income Statement + Balance Sheet)","Budget forecasts and performance reports","Cost sheets and variance analysis"],1,"The output of accounting is Financial Statements — Income Statement (profit/loss) and Balance Sheet (financial position).","Ebook","Other options: Budget forecasts are output of Management Accounting, not Financial Accounting · Tax returns are a use of accounting data, not the primary output · Cost sheets are output of Cost Accounting, not the general accounting system"],
[1,"Accounting is sometimes called the 'Language of Financial Decisions' — NOT the 'Language of Business'. Why?",["Because accounting only deals with financial institutions","Because business includes non-financial aspects that accounting does not capture","Because accounting is used only by finance professionals","Because business decisions are always quantitative"],1,"Business includes non-financial aspects (employee morale, brand value, MD's health) that accounting cannot capture. Hence 'Language of Financial Decisions' is more precise.","Ebook","Other options: Some may confuse this with 'Language of Business' — the notes explicitly say Financial Decisions, not Business · Non-monetary facts are excluded under the Money Measurement concept · The distinction is about scope — financial vs all aspects of business"],
[1,"Which branch of accounting focuses on EXTERNAL reporting and produces the Income Statement and Balance Sheet?",["Cost Accounting","Management Accounting","Financial Accounting","Tax Accounting"],2,"Financial Accounting focuses on external reporting of all transactions and produces the Income Statement and Balance Sheet.","Ebook","Other options: Management Accounting is for internal decision-making only · Cost Accounting produces cost sheets, not financial statements · Financial Accounting is historical/past-focused — not future-oriented"],
[1,"Which of the following is a key feature of Management Accounting that distinguishes it from Financial Accounting?",["It follows GAAP strictly","It is prepared for external users like banks and creditors","It is forward-looking and future-oriented","It produces Income Statement and Balance Sheet"],2,"Management Accounting is forward-looking (future-oriented), flexible in format, and prepared exclusively for internal managers.","Ebook","Other options: Financial Accounting follows GAAP — Management Accounting does not · Management Accounting is for internal users only, not external · Financial Accounting is past-focused; Management is future-focused"],
[1,"Under which basis of accounting is revenue recognised when EARNED (not when cash is received)?",["Cash Basis","Accrual / Mercantile Basis","Hybrid Basis","Cost Basis"],1,"Under Accrual / Mercantile Basis, revenue is recognised when earned and expenses when incurred — regardless of cash movement.","Ebook","Other options: Cash basis records revenue only when cash is actually received · Accrual basis is the preferred and exam-important basis · Accrual basis gives a complete picture of profit/loss — cash basis does not"],
[1,"Under the Cash Basis of accounting, which of the following items would NOT be recorded?",["Cash sales of ₹3,90,000","Cash expenses paid of ₹1,90,000","Credit sales of ₹1,60,000","Cash purchases of ₹50,000"],2,"Cash basis records only actual cash transactions. Credit sales (not yet received in cash) are excluded — they would be included under accrual basis.","Ebook","Other options: Cash sales are included in both bases · Outstanding expenses are also excluded under cash basis · Accrual basis includes credit sales AND outstanding expenses"],
[1,"Which type of account covers Goodwill A/c and Patent A/c?",["Personal Accounts (Artificial)","Real Accounts (Intangible)","Nominal Accounts","Real Accounts (Tangible)"],1,"Real Accounts cover both tangible assets (Cash, Building) and intangible assets (Goodwill, Patent). These are asset accounts.","Ebook","Other options: Goodwill and Patents are intangible real accounts — not nominal accounts · Nominal accounts cover expenses and income (Salary, Rent, Interest Received) · Personal accounts cover persons — natural, artificial (Bank A/c), or representative"],
[1,"Outstanding Salary A/c is classified under which type of account?",["Nominal Account","Real Account","Personal Account (Representative)","Personal Account (Artificial)"],2,"Outstanding Salary A/c is a Representative Personal Account — it represents persons (employees) to whom salary is owed but not yet paid.","Ebook","Other options: Salary A/c itself is Nominal — but Outstanding Salary A/c is Representative Personal · Representative personal accounts represent groups of persons, not named individuals · Artificial personal accounts are entities like Bank, Company, Government"],
[1,"What is the Golden Rule for Personal Accounts?",["Debit what comes in; Credit what goes out","Debit all expenses & losses; Credit all incomes & gains","Debit the receiver; Credit the giver","Debit assets; Credit liabilities"],2,"Personal accounts rule: Debit the Receiver, Credit the Giver. For example, when you pay an employee salary, they are the receiver (Dr their account) and cash goes out.","Ebook","Other options: 'Debit what comes in, Credit what goes out' is the rule for Real Accounts · 'Debit expenses, Credit incomes' is the rule for Nominal Accounts · This rule applies to natural, artificial, and representative personal accounts"],
[1,"What is the Golden Rule for Real Accounts?",["Debit the receiver; Credit the giver","Debit all expenses & losses; Credit all incomes & gains","Debit what comes in; Credit what goes out","Debit assets; Credit capital"],2,"Real Accounts rule: Debit what comes in, Credit what goes out. For example, when cash comes in, Cash A/c is debited; when machinery goes out, Machinery A/c is credited.","Ebook","Other options: Real accounts = accounts of assets (tangible + intangible) · 'Debit the receiver, Credit the giver' is for Personal Accounts · This applies even to intangible assets like Goodwill"],
[1,"Which accounting concept states that assets must be recorded at their HISTORICAL PURCHASE PRICE, not market value?",["Going Concern Concept","Cost Concept","Realisation Concept","Matching Concept"],1,"The Cost Concept states that assets are recorded at their historical purchase price (cost = acquisition + transport + installation), not current market value.","Ebook","Other options: Going Concern assumes business will continue — justifies depreciation · Realisation concept deals with when revenue is recognised (at point of sale) · Cost concept is why balance sheet shows acquisition cost, not current value"],
[1,"The Matching Concept requires that expenses must be:",["Paid in the same period they are incurred","Matched with the revenue of the same period they helped generate","Recorded when cash is paid","Disclosed in the notes to financial statements"],1,"Matching Concept: expenses of a period must be matched against the revenue they helped generate in that same period — regardless of when cash moves.","Ebook","Other options: Matching is about revenue-expense alignment in the same period, not cash timing · This concept underpins the accrual basis of accounting · Disclosure is a convention, not part of the Matching concept"],
[1,"Which accounting concept forms the basis of the Balance Sheet equation Assets = Liabilities + Capital?",["Business Entity Concept","Money Measurement Concept","Dual Aspect Concept","Going Concern Concept"],2,"The Dual Aspect Concept states every transaction has two effects and forms the basis of double-entry accounting: Assets = Liabilities + Capital.","Ebook","Other options: Business Entity separates owner from business — doesn't directly create the equation · Going Concern is about continuity of business, not the equation · Dual aspect = every debit has a corresponding credit"],
[1,"Which accounting concept justifies the calculation of depreciation on fixed assets?",["Matching Concept","Cost Concept","Going Concern Concept","Accounting Period Concept"],2,"Going Concern assumes the business will continue indefinitely — this justifies spreading the cost of fixed assets (depreciation) over their useful lives.","Ebook","Other options: If Going Concern fails, assets would be valued at liquidation price, not cost · Matching concept relates expenses to revenues, not specifically to depreciation methodology · Accounting Period concept divides business life into intervals — used for periodic reporting"],
[1,"Which accounting concept requires that revenue is recognised ONLY at the point of sale when legal liability to pay arises?",["Accrual Concept","Matching Concept","Realisation Concept","Consistency Concept"],2,"Realisation Concept: revenue is recognised at the point of sale — when property passes to the buyer and a legal liability to pay arises.","Ebook","Other options: Accrual concept is about income/expense timing (earned/incurred, not cash) · Realisation is specifically about the trigger for revenue recognition — the sale event · Do not confuse Accrual (timing) with Realisation (trigger for revenue)"],
[1,"Which accounting convention states 'Anticipate losses, don't anticipate gains'?",["Consistency","Materiality","Conservatism (Prudence)","Disclosure"],2,"Conservatism / Prudence convention: anticipate all possible losses but do not anticipate gains — be cautious and err on the side of caution.","Ebook","Other options: Consistency requires using same methods year-to-year for comparability · Materiality deals with disclosing items significant enough to affect decisions · Disclosure requires all relevant information to be disclosed — not about caution"],
[1,"Which accounting convention is violated if a company changes its depreciation method every year?",["Conservatism","Consistency","Materiality","Realisation"],1,"Consistency convention requires the same accounting methods to be used year-to-year for comparability. Changing depreciation method every year violates this.","Ebook","Other options: Consistency enables year-on-year comparability of financial statements · Conservatism is about anticipating losses — unrelated to method changes · Materiality is about what needs to be disclosed — not about consistency of methods"],
[1,"The Business Entity Concept means:",["Business must register as a legal entity","Owner's personal transactions are excluded from business records","Business income and personal income are taxed together","Business must have a separate bank account"],1,"Business Entity Concept: the business is treated as separate from its owner. Transactions are recorded from the business's perspective. Owner's capital = liability OF the business.","Ebook","Other options: Owner's capital is a liability of the business — not an asset · This concept applies regardless of legal form (even sole proprietorship) · Business Entity is an accounting concept — not a legal requirement"],
[1,"Which of the following would NOT be recorded in accounting under the Money Measurement Concept?",["Purchase of machinery for ₹5,00,000","Loss on sale of investment ₹20,000","Employee morale and team spirit","Outstanding rent payable ₹15,000"],2,"Money Measurement Concept: only transactions expressible in money terms are recorded. Employee morale, MD's health, team spirit — these are non-monetary and excluded.","Ebook","Other options: All monetary transactions (purchases, losses, outstanding amounts) are recorded · Non-monetary qualitative facts are excluded — regardless of their importance · This is one reason accounting has limitations — non-financial factors are invisible"],
[1,"In comparing Financial Accounting vs Management Accounting, what is the Time Focus of Financial Accounting?",["Future-oriented (forecasts and budgets)","Current period only","Past (historical transactions)","Both past and future equally"],2,"Financial Accounting is past-focused — it records and reports historical transactions. Management Accounting is future-oriented for decision-making.","Ebook","Other options: Management Accounting is future-oriented — Financial is historical · Financial Accounting follows GAAP; Management does not have rigid principles · Financial Accounting serves external users; Management serves internal managers"],
[1,"Which of the following is an EXTERNAL user of accounting information?",["Managers setting goals","Employees deciding on profit-sharing","Owners assessing business performance","SEBI for regulatory compliance"],3,"SEBI (and government bodies) are external users. They use accounting information for tax computation and regulatory compliance.","Ebook","Other options: Managers, Employees, Owners are all internal users · Banks are external users — they make lending decisions based on financials · Consumers and Researchers are also external users"],
[1,"The Accounting Period Concept states that:",["All transactions must be recorded within 30 days","Business life is divided into periodic intervals (usually 1 year) for reporting","Accounts must be audited at the end of every quarter","Only annual transactions are considered for accounting"],1,"Accounting Period Concept: business life is divided into periodic intervals (typically 1 year) to enable periodic reporting and performance comparison.","Ebook","Other options: Period can be less than a year for interim reporting — it doesn't have to be annual only · This concept enables the Matching Concept to work (matching within periods) · Don't confuse with audit requirements — this is an accounting principle, not an audit rule"],
[2,"According to L.C. Cropper, the Journal is defined as:",["A book that records only cash transactions in chronological order","A book employed to classify or sort out transactions in a form convenient for their subsequent entries in Ledger","A final statement showing profit and loss for the year","A book that records all credit transactions of a business"],1,"L.C. Cropper's definition: Journal is a book employed to classify/sort transactions conveniently for subsequent ledger entries. It is the Book of Original Entry.","Ebook","Other options: Journal records ALL transactions, not just cash — cash transactions go to Cash Book in large businesses · Journal is NOT a final statement — it's the first step (Book of Original Entry) · Attribution matters: L.C. Cropper defines Journal; Eric Kohler defines Trial Balance"],
[2,"The word 'JOURNAL' is derived from the French word 'JOUR' which means:",["Money","Record","Day","Book"],2,"Journal comes from French 'JOUR' = Day, reflecting that it records day-to-day (daily) transactions in chronological order.","Ebook","Other options: Journal = daily record — chronological order is essential · Petty Cash Book's name 'Petty' comes from French 'Petit' = small (a different French word) · This etymology explains why journal records transactions date-wise"],
[2,"The Journal is also called the 'Book of Original Entry'. Which step in the accounting cycle does it represent?",["Final step — after Trial Balance","Second step — after Ledger","First step — before Ledger","Third step — after Final Accounts"],2,"Journal is the FIRST step. The accounting cycle flows: Journal → Ledger → Trial Balance → Final Accounts. Journal captures transactions first.","Ebook","Other options: Journal comes BEFORE Ledger — it's the Book of Original/Prime Entry · Ledger is posted FROM the Journal — not the other way around · Trial Balance and Final Accounts come after Journal + Ledger"],
[2,"How many columns does a standard Journal format have?",["3 columns","4 columns","5 columns","6 columns"],2,"Journal has 5 columns: Date | Particulars | L.F. (Ledger Folio) | Debit Amount | Credit Amount.","Ebook","Other options: L.F. column is a key column students often forget — it links to the Ledger page number · Narration is part of the Particulars column — not a separate column · The 5-column format is standard for all journal entries"],
[2,"What does L.F. stand for in the Journal format?",["Last Figure","Ledger Folio","Legal Format","Ledger Form"],1,"L.F. = Ledger Folio — the page number in the Ledger where this journal entry is posted. Written in red ink.","Ebook","Other options: Folio = page number — L.F. is the cross-reference to the Ledger · L.F. is filled in when the entry is posted to the Ledger (not when journalised) · This provides an audit trail between Journal and Ledger"],
[2,"In the Journal's Particulars column, the Credit account is prefixed with which word?",["By","Being","To","From"],2,"In the Particulars column, the Credit account is written on the second line prefixed with 'To'. The narration below it starts with 'Being'.","Ebook","Other options: 'To' prefixes the Credit account in Particulars · 'By' is used in Cash Book (credit side) and Ledger (credit side) — NOT in Journal · 'Being' starts the narration — it is NOT a prefix for the credit account"],
[2,"What is 'Journalising'?",["The process of balancing ledger accounts","The process of entering/recording transaction data in the Journal","The process of preparing the Trial Balance","The process of preparing Final Accounts"],1,"Journalising = the process of entering/recording transaction data in the Journal. It is done by software like Tally today.","Ebook","Other options: Journalising is only about recording in the Journal — not Ledger or TB · Balancing is done in the Ledger — not at the Journal stage · The output of journalising is a journal entry, not a final account"],
[2,"What does 'Casting' of Journal mean?",["Transferring entries from Journal to Ledger","Totalling the Debit and Credit columns at end of each page to verify they are equal","Opening a new set of books at start of financial year","Writing the narration for each journal entry"],1,"Casting = totalling the Debit and Credit columns at end of each Journal page. Total Dr must equal Total Cr. Balances carried forward as C/F and B/F.","Ebook","Other options: Casting is an arithmetical check — Dr total = Cr total at page end · C/F = Carried Forward (bottom of page); B/F = Brought Forward (top of next page) · Casting ≠ Posting (which is transfer to Ledger)"],
[2,"In an Opening Entry, which side are Assets recorded on?",["Credit side","Debit side","Both sides equally","Opening entries don't record assets"],1,"Opening Entry: Assets → Debited (debit balance). Liabilities + Capital → Credited. Closing balance of previous year becomes opening balance of new year.","Ebook","Other options: Assets always have debit balance — they are debited in Opening Entry · Capital is credited (credit balance) in Opening Entry · Opening Entry is the first entry in new books / new financial year"],
[2,"A Compound Journal Entry is one that:",["Has exactly 1 debit and 1 credit","Involves more than 2 accounts (multiple debits and/or credits)","Is made at compound interest","Combines two journals into one book"],1,"Compound Entry: involves more than 2 accounts (e.g., buying a building part cash + part loan = Building Dr; Cash Cr + Notes Payable Cr). Simple entry has exactly 2 accounts.","Ebook","Other options: Simple entry = 1 Dr + 1 Cr (exactly 2 accounts) · Compound entry = more than 2 accounts — multiple Drs and/or Credits · Both simple and compound entries must have equal Dr and Cr totals"],
[2,"Sunil started business with cash ₹25,000. Which accounts are affected and how?",["Capital A/c Dr; Cash A/c Cr","Cash A/c Dr; Capital A/c Cr","Bank A/c Dr; Capital A/c Cr","Cash A/c Dr; Revenue A/c Cr"],1,"Cash A/c (Real) Dr — cash comes in (what comes in). Capital A/c (Personal) Cr — owner is the giver (credit the giver).","Ebook","Other options: Capital A/c is a Personal Account — owner's equity in the business · Cash comes IN → Real account rule: Debit what comes in · Owner brings in capital → he is the giver → Credit the giver"],
[2,"Sunil paid electricity charges ₹900 in cash. Which journal entry is correct?",["Cash A/c Dr; Electricity A/c Cr","Electricity A/c Dr; Cash A/c Cr","Electricity A/c Dr; Capital A/c Cr","Bank A/c Dr; Electricity A/c Cr"],1,"Electricity A/c (Nominal) Dr — expense, debit all expenses. Cash A/c (Real) Cr — cash goes out, credit what goes out.","Ebook","Other options: Electricity is a Nominal account (expense) — Debit all expenses · Cash goes out → Real account rule: Credit what goes out · If paid by cheque, Bank A/c would be Cr instead of Cash A/c"],
[2,"Sunil purchased goods from Z on credit for ₹10,000. What is debited?",["Z A/c","Sales A/c","Purchases A/c","Cash A/c"],2,"Purchases A/c (Real) Dr — goods come in (what comes in). Z A/c (Personal) Cr — Z is the giver of goods (credit the giver).","Ebook","Other options: Purchases A/c is debited — not Cash (it's a credit purchase, no cash moves) · Z A/c is credited — Z gave the goods on credit, so Z is the giver · This is a credit purchase — it goes in Purchases Day Book in larger businesses"],
[2,"Debit increases which of the following?",["Liabilities and Revenue","Assets and Expenses","Equity and Revenue","Capital and Liabilities"],1,"Debit increases Assets and Expenses. Credit increases Liabilities, Revenue, and Equity (Capital).","Ebook","Other options: DEAD CLIC mnemonic: Debit = Expenses, Assets, Drawings; Credit = Liabilities, Income, Capital · Revenue/Income is increased by Credit — not Debit · Capital is a Credit balance account — credit increases it"],
[2,"Which of the following is NOT an advantage of maintaining a Journal?",["Provides chronological record of all transactions","Narration documents the reason for each entry","Eliminates the need for a Ledger","Minimises errors as Dr must equal Cr for each entry"],2,"Journal does NOT eliminate the need for a Ledger. Journal → Ledger → Trial Balance is the sequence. Both are needed. Journal feeds into the Ledger.","Ebook","Other options: Journal's advantage is it acts as the basis for Ledger — not a replacement · Ledger stays concise because details are in the Journal · Journal is a source document; Ledger classifies by account"],
[2,"Sunil paid salary ₹1,000 by cheque. Which account is CREDITED?",["Salary A/c","Cash A/c","Bank A/c","Capital A/c"],2,"Salary A/c (Nominal) Dr — expense. Bank A/c (Personal — Artificial) Cr — bank is the giver of payment (credit the giver).","Ebook","Other options: Payment by cheque = Bank A/c is credited (not Cash A/c) · Payment by cash = Cash A/c is credited · Salary A/c is always debited — it is an expense (Nominal account rule)"],
[2,"The Golden Rule for Nominal Accounts is:",["Debit the receiver; Credit the giver","Debit what comes in; Credit what goes out","Debit all expenses & losses; Credit all incomes & gains","Debit all gains; Credit all expenses"],2,"Nominal Account rule: Debit all expenses & losses; Credit all incomes & gains. Examples: Salary (expense) → Dr; Commission Received (income) → Cr.","Ebook","Other options: Nominal accounts = expenses, losses, incomes, gains · 'Debit the receiver, Credit the giver' is Personal Account rule · 'Debit what comes in, Credit what goes out' is Real Account rule"],
[2,"Which of the following characteristics correctly describes a Journal?",["Records transactions account-wise (ledger-style)","Records transactions chronologically with both debit and credit sides shown","Records only credit transactions","Replaces the need for Final Accounts"],1,"Journal records transactions chronologically (date-wise), showing both Debit and Credit sides for every transaction, with narration.","Ebook","Other options: Ledger records account-wise — Journal records date-wise (chronologically) · Journal records ALL transactions (cash and credit) — not just credit · Journal is the beginning of accounting cycle, not a replacement for Final Accounts"],
[2,"When Sunil purchased machinery for cash ₹8,000, what is the entry?",["Cash A/c Dr; Machinery A/c Cr","Purchases A/c Dr; Cash A/c Cr","Machinery A/c Dr; Cash A/c Cr","Capital A/c Dr; Machinery A/c Cr"],2,"Machinery A/c (Real) Dr — machinery comes in (debit what comes in). Cash A/c (Real) Cr — cash goes out (credit what goes out).","Ebook","Other options: Machinery is a Real Asset account — not a Purchases account · Purchases A/c is used for goods bought for resale — not for fixed assets · Both Machinery and Cash are Real accounts — apply 'in/out' rule to both"],
[2,"What are the 3 steps to determine Debit and Credit for any transaction?",["Record → Post → Balance","Identify accounts → Classify account type → Apply Golden Rule","Journalise → Ledger → Trial Balance","Debit assets → Credit liabilities → Balance both"],1,"The 3 steps: (1) Identify the two accounts involved, (2) Classify each as Personal/Real/Nominal, (3) Apply the Golden Rule for each account type.","Ebook","Other options: Classification of account type (step 2) is critical — wrong classification = wrong entry · Every transaction affects exactly 2 accounts in simple entries · Golden Rules apply differently to each account type"],
[2,"Narration in a Journal entry begins with which word?",["To","By","Being","For"],2,"Narration is a brief explanation of the journal entry written in brackets, beginning with 'Being'. Example: (Being salary paid for the month of April).","Ebook","Other options: 'To' prefixes the Credit account in Particulars column — not the narration · 'By' is used in Ledger/Cash Book credit side — not in Journal narration · Narration is mandatory — it documents the reason for the entry"],
[2,"What does 'Total C/F' written at the bottom of a Journal page mean?",["Cash Flow total for the period","Total Carried Forward — sum carried to the top of the next page","Central Fund total","Total Confirmed and Filed"],1,"C/F = Carried Forward. The running total of Debit and Credit columns is carried forward to the top of the next page as B/F (Brought Forward).","Ebook","Other options: B/F = Brought Forward (appears at top of next page) · C/F = Carried Forward (appears at bottom of current page) · Grand Total is cast on the LAST page of the journal"],
[3,"What are Subsidiary Books?",["Books that replace the Journal entirely in large businesses","Books of prime entry where transactions of a particular/similar category are recorded separately","Summary books prepared after the Trial Balance","Books maintained by subsidiary companies"],1,"Subsidiary Books = Books of prime entry where transactions of a similar/particular category are recorded separately. They are sub-divisions of the Journal.","Ebook","Other options: Subsidiary Books don't replace the Journal — they are a specialised sub-division of it · Small businesses use a single Journal; large businesses sub-divide it · Also called: Special Journals / Day Books / Books of Original Entry"],
[3,"Which of the following is NOT another name for Subsidiary Books?",["Special Journals","Day Books","Ledger Accounts","Books of Prime Entry"],2,"Ledger Accounts is NOT another name for Subsidiary Books. Subsidiary Books are also called Special Journals, Day Books, Books of Original Entry, or Books of Prime Entry.","Ebook","Other options: Ledger = classified account-wise record; Subsidiary Books = categorised journal · Day Books and Special Journals are synonyms for Subsidiary Books · Subsidiary Books feed INTO the Ledger — they are not ledger accounts"],
[3,"How many main types of Subsidiary Books are there?",["3","4","5","6"],2,"There are 5 main Subsidiary Books: Purchase Book, Purchase Return Book, Sales Book, Sales Return Book, and Cash Book.","Ebook","Other options: The 5 are: Purchase, Purchase Return, Sales, Sales Return, Cash Book · Cash Book is the most important — it has dual nature (Journal + Ledger) · Don't forget Purchase Return and Sales Return as separate books"],
[3,"The Purchase Book records which type of purchases?",["All purchases — cash and credit","Cash purchases of goods only","Credit purchases of goods only","Credit purchases of goods and assets"],2,"Purchase Book records CREDIT purchases of GOODS only. Cash purchases go to Cash Book. Credit purchases of non-goods (furniture, equipment) go to Journal.","Ebook","Other options: Cash purchases are NOT in Purchase Book — they go to Cash Book · Credit purchase of furniture/equipment goes to Journal — not Purchase Book · Only GOODS purchased on credit qualify for Purchase Book"],
[3,"What document is written from when recording in the Purchase Book?",["Outward Invoice","Credit Note","Debit Note","Inward Invoice"],3,"Purchase Book entries are written from the Inward Invoice — received along with the goods from the supplier.","Ebook","Other options: Inward Invoice = received by buyer from supplier (for Purchase Book) · Outward Invoice = sent by seller to buyer (for Sales Book) · Debit Note is used for Purchase Returns — not for Purchase Book itself"],
[3,"The Purchase Book is totalled monthly and posted to which account?",["Sales A/c (Credit)","Purchases A/c (Debit)","Creditors A/c (Debit)","Cash A/c (Debit)"],1,"The monthly total of Purchase Book is posted to Purchases A/c (Dr). Individual supplier accounts are credited separately.","Ebook","Other options: Purchase Book total → Purchases A/c Debit (goods came in) · Individual supplier accounts → Credited (each supplier is the giver) · Not Sales A/c — that's for Sales Book"],
[3,"What is the net value formula used in the Purchase Book?",["Invoice Price + Trade Discount","Invoice Price − Trade Discount","Invoice Price + Cash Discount","Invoice Price − Cash Discount"],1,"Net Value = Invoice Price − Trade Discount. Trade discount is deducted before recording; cash discount is NOT deducted at purchase time.","Ebook","Other options: Trade Discount is deducted immediately — not a separate account · Cash Discount is only given when payment is made — not at time of purchase · Net value = what is actually payable after trade discount"],
[3,"When a buyer returns goods to the supplier, what document does the BUYER prepare?",["Credit Note","Inward Invoice","Debit Note","Outward Invoice"],2,"Buyer prepares a Debit Note when returning goods to the supplier. The supplier then sends back a Credit Note to acknowledge the return.","Ebook","Other options: Debit Note → prepared by Buyer (for purchases returned) · Credit Note → prepared by Seller/Supplier (acknowledging return) · In Sales Returns: Credit Note is prepared by the Seller (your business)"],
[3,"The Purchase Return Book is also called:",["Returns Inward Book","Returns Outward Book","Outward Sales Book","Reverse Purchase Ledger"],1,"Purchase Return Book = Returns Outward Book. Returns go OUTWARD from the business back to suppliers. Sales Return Book = Returns Inward Book.","Ebook","Other options: Returns Outward = goods going OUT back to supplier = Purchase Returns · Returns Inward = goods coming IN from customers = Sales Returns · Confusing inward/outward is a common exam trap"],
[3,"The Sales Book records which transactions?",["All sales — cash and credit","Credit sales of goods only","Cash sales of goods only","Credit sales of all items including assets"],1,"Sales Book records CREDIT sales of GOODS only. Cash sales → Cash Book. Credit sales of non-goods assets → Journal.","Ebook","Other options: Cash sales go to Cash Book — not Sales Book · Credit sale of old machinery → Journal (not Sales Book) · Only goods sold on credit qualify for Sales Book"],
[3,"The total of the Sales Book is posted to which account?",["Purchases A/c (Dr)","Cash A/c (Cr)","Sales A/c (Cr)","Debtors A/c (Cr)"],2,"Sales Book total → Sales A/c (Cr). Individual customer accounts are Debited (customers received goods on credit — they are receivers).","Ebook","Other options: Sales A/c is always Credited — goods went out, income was earned · Individual customer accounts are Debited — they are receivers · Don't confuse: Purchase Book total → Purchases Dr; Sales Book total → Sales Cr"],
[3,"When goods are returned BY the customer, which document does the SELLER prepare?",["Debit Note","Inward Invoice","Purchase Return voucher","Credit Note"],3,"When customer returns goods, the Seller prepares a Credit Note. The buyer/customer sends a Debit Note. Sales Return Book = Returns Inward Book.","Ebook","Other options: In Sales Returns: Credit Note by Seller (your business) · In Purchase Returns: Debit Note by Buyer (your business) · The preparer switches depending on who is returning goods"],
[3,"The Cash Book has a dual nature. It functions as:",["Journal only — not a Ledger","Ledger only — not a Journal","Both Journal (Book of Original Entry) AND Ledger (Cash Account)","Neither Journal nor Ledger — it is a separate category"],2,"Cash Book has dual nature: it is both a Journal (book of original entry for cash transactions) AND a Ledger (no separate Cash A/c needed in ledger).","Ebook","Other options: Because Cash Book acts as Ledger, no separate Cash A/c is needed in the Ledger · This is a unique feature of Cash Book — other subsidiary books don't have dual nature · Cash Book is the most important subsidiary book"],
[3,"How many types of Cash Book are there?",["2","3","4","5"],2,"There are 4 types of Cash Book: Single Column (Simple), Double Column, Triple Column, and Petty Cash Book.","Ebook","Other options: 4 types: Single, Double, Triple column + Petty Cash Book · Single = Cash only; Double = Cash + Bank; Triple = Cash + Bank + Discount · Petty Cash Book is for small/petty expenses maintained by Petty Cashier"],
[3,"In the Triple Column Cash Book, the Discount column is:",["Balanced like other columns","Totalled only — NOT balanced","Ignored in final accounts","Posted as a single lump sum to Cash A/c"],1,"The Discount column in a Triple Column Cash Book is TOTALLED but NOT BALANCED — because it is not an account by itself. It is posted to Discount Allowed/Received A/c.","Ebook","Other options: Only Cash and Bank columns are balanced — Discount column is only totalled · Discount Allowed (Dr side) = expense; Discount Received (Cr side) = income · Triple column has 7 columns on each side"],
[3,"The word 'Petty' in Petty Cash Book comes from which language, and means what?",["Latin: 'Pettus' = minor","French: 'Petit' = small","German: 'Pettas' = cash","English: 'Petty' = insignificant"],1,"'Petty' comes from French 'Petit' = small. Petty Cash Book records small/petty expenses like stationery, postage, and refreshments.","Ebook","Other options: French origin: Journal = Jour (day), Petty = Petit (small) · Petty Cashier maintains the Petty Cash Book — separate from Main Cashier · Amount is advanced to Petty Cashier at start of period by Main Cashier"],
[3,"In the Cash Book, the Debit side (receipts) Particulars column entries start with which word?",["By","Being","To","For"],2,"In Cash Book: Debit side (receipts) Particulars starts with 'To'. Credit side (payments) Particulars starts with 'By'.","Ebook","Other options: Cash Book Debit (receipt) = 'To'; Cash Book Credit (payment) = 'By' · Contrast with Journal: Particulars uses 'To' for Credit account only · In Ledger: Debit side = 'To'; Credit side = 'By' (same as Cash Book)"],
[3,"The opening balance in a Cash Book is shown as:",["'By Balance b/d' on the credit side","'To Balance b/d' on the debit side","'To Balance c/d' on the credit side","'By Balance c/d' on the debit side"],1,"Opening balance in Cash Book = 'To Balance b/d' on the debit (receipt) side. Closing balance = 'By Balance c/d' on the credit side.","Ebook","Other options: b/d = brought down (opening); c/d = carried down (closing) · Opening balance is always on DEBIT side for Cash Book (positive cash = asset = debit) · Bank overdraft is an exception — it would show on credit side"],
[3,"A Bank Overdraft appears in the Double Column Cash Book as:",["Debit balance in Bank column","Credit balance shown as 'To Balance c/d' on the receipt/debit side","A separate column for overdraft","It cannot be shown in Cash Book"],1,"Bank Overdraft = Credit side > Debit side in bank column. The credit balance is brought down as 'To Balance c/d' on the receipt (debit) side of next period.","Ebook","Other options: Normal bank balance = Debit (more receipts than payments) · Overdraft = bank owes more than what we have = Credit balance in bank column · Bank A/c is a Personal Account — can have Dr or Cr (overdraft) balance"],
[3,"Which Subsidiary Book helps in DIVISION OF LABOUR among staff?",["Only the Cash Book","All Subsidiary Books — each handled by a different staff member","Only Purchase and Sales Books","Only the Petty Cash Book"],1,"All Subsidiary Books enable division of labour — different staff members handle different books (purchases, sales, cash, returns), improving efficiency.","Ebook","Other options: Division of labour is one of the key reasons subsidiary books exist · It also provides internal check — one clerk's book is verified by another · This benefit applies to ALL subsidiary books, not just some"],
[3,"Credit purchases of STATIONERY (not goods for resale) should be recorded in:",["Purchase Book","Sales Book","Journal Proper","Cash Book"],2,"Credit purchase of stationery (a non-goods item) goes to Journal Proper — NOT to Purchase Book. Purchase Book is ONLY for credit purchases of trading goods.","Ebook","Other options: Purchase Book = credit purchases of GOODS for resale only · Furniture, equipment, stationery bought on credit → Journal Proper · Cash purchase of stationery → Cash Book (not Journal or Purchase Book)"],
[3,"Sales Return Book is also known as:",["Returns Outward Book","Returns Inward Book","Reverse Sales Journal","Credit Purchase Book"],1,"Sales Return Book = Returns Inward Book (goods return INWARD from customer back to the business). Purchase Return = Returns Outward (goods go outward to supplier).","Ebook","Other options: Returns Inward = Sales Returns (goods come back in from customers) · Returns Outward = Purchase Returns (goods go out back to suppliers) · Inward/Outward is from the business's perspective"],
[3,"The Outward Invoice (sent with goods to the buyer) is used as source document for which book?",["Purchase Book","Cash Book","Sales Book","Purchase Return Book"],2,"Sales Book is written from the Outward Invoice — the invoice sent TO the buyer along with goods sold on credit.","Ebook","Other options: Outward Invoice → Sales Book (goods go out = sales) · Inward Invoice → Purchase Book (goods come in = purchases) · Don't mix up inward/outward invoice direction"],
[3,"The Purchase Return Book total is periodically posted to:",["Purchases A/c (Dr)","Purchase Returns A/c (Cr)","Sales A/c (Cr)","Creditors A/c (Dr)"],1,"Purchase Return Book total → Purchase Returns A/c (Credit). This reduces the purchases amount. Individual supplier accounts are Debited.","Ebook","Other options: Returns reduce purchases — hence Purchase Returns A/c is Credited · Supplier accounts are Debited when goods are returned to them · Net Purchases = Purchases − Purchase Returns"],
[4,"Who defined the Trial Balance as 'a list or abstract of the balances or of total debits and total credits of accounts in a ledger'?",["L.C. Cropper","Eric Kohler","Adam Smith","Luca Pacioli"],1,"Eric Kohler defined the Trial Balance. Recall: L.C. Cropper defined the Journal. Knowing which authority defined which concept is a common exam question.","Ebook","Other options: L.C. Cropper → Journal definition · Eric Kohler → Trial Balance definition · Luca Pacioli is credited with the double-entry bookkeeping system — not Trial Balance"],
[4,"The Trial Balance is also called:",["Statement of Accounts","Statement of Balance","Statement of Affairs","Balance of Payments"],1,"Trial Balance is also called 'Statement of Balance'. It lists debit and credit balances of all ledger accounts.","Ebook","Other options: Statement of Affairs is related to insolvency/liquidation — different context · Balance of Payments is a macroeconomic term — not accounting terminology · Trial Balance = Statement of Balance = list of all ledger account balances"],
[4,"Where does the Trial Balance fit in the accounting cycle?",["After Final Accounts","Before the Journal","After Ledger, before Final Accounts","Simultaneously with Journal entries"],2,"Accounting cycle: Journal → Ledger → Trial Balance → Final Accounts. Trial Balance is prepared AFTER Ledger balancing and BEFORE preparing Final Accounts.","Ebook","Other options: TB is the link between Ledger and Final Accounts · TB is NOT prepared before Journal or Ledger · Final Accounts (Trading A/c, P&L, Balance Sheet) are prepared FROM Trial Balance"],
[4,"If the Total Debit = Total Credit in a Trial Balance, it means:",["There are absolutely no errors in the books","Arithmetical accuracy is confirmed but it is NOT conclusive proof of error-free books","Final Accounts can be prepared immediately without review","All ledger accounts have been correctly balanced"],1,"TB agreement confirms arithmetical/mathematical accuracy — but it is NOT conclusive proof. Certain errors (wrong account, omission, compensating, principle) are NOT detected.","Ebook","Other options: Agreement of TB ≠ No errors. TB can agree even with errors present · 4 types of errors not disclosed: wrong account, omission, compensating, principle · This is a major limitation of Trial Balance"],
[4,"Which of the following errors would NOT be detected by the Trial Balance?",["Posting ₹500 to Debit but ₹250 to Credit","Recording a transaction to a completely wrong account but with correct amount","Writing a Debit entry but forgetting the Credit","Adding the Debit column incorrectly"],1,"Error of wrong account: correct amount posted, but to the wrong account. Both sides still balance. TB cannot detect this — it is called an 'Error of Commission'.","Ebook","Other options: Wrong account errors → TB still agrees (correct total, wrong account) · Omission errors (not recorded at all) → TB still agrees · Compensating errors (two opposite errors cancelling) → TB still agrees"],
[4,"When a transaction is NOT recorded at all in the books, what type of error is it?",["Error of Commission","Error of Principle","Compensating Error","Error of Omission"],3,"Error of Omission: transaction completely omitted/not recorded. Since both debit and credit are missed, the TB still agrees — this error is NOT disclosed by TB.","Ebook","Other options: Omission = both Dr and Cr are missing → TB still agrees · Commission = wrong account used → amounts correct, TB agrees · Compensating = two errors that offset each other → TB agrees"],
[4,"How many main objectives does the Trial Balance serve?",["2","3","4","5"],2,"4 objectives: (1) Check arithmetical accuracy, (2) Locate accounting errors, (3) Summarise financial transactions, (4) Base for Final Accounts.","Ebook","Other options: All 4 objectives should be memorised — exam may ask for the number · Key objective is arithmetical check — but remember it's not conclusive · TB acts as the bridge between Ledger and Final Accounts"],
[4,"In the Journal (Vertical) Format of Trial Balance, which items appear on the Debit side?",["Liabilities, Income, Capital","Assets, Expenses, Losses","Assets, Liabilities, Capital","Expenses, Income, Capital"],1,"Debit side of TB: Assets, Expenses, Losses. Credit side: Liabilities, Income, Gains, Capital.","Ebook","Other options: Assets always have debit balance → Debit side of TB · Capital has credit balance → Credit side of TB · Income/Revenue has credit balance → Credit side"],
[4,"Which method of preparing Trial Balance uses CLOSING BALANCES of ledger accounts?",["Total Method","Gross Method","Balance Method","Net Method"],2,"Balance Method uses closing balances of ledger accounts — it is the more commonly used method in practice. Total Method uses totals without balancing.","Ebook","Other options: Balance Method = more common; Total Method = accounts not balanced · Total Method: Grand total Dr = Grand total Cr · Balance Method shows net position (Dr balance or Cr balance) per account"],
[4,"Which is the more commonly used type of Trial Balance in practice?",["Gross Trial Balance","Net Trial Balance","Total Trial Balance","Adjusted Trial Balance"],1,"Net Trial Balance (shows only the balance — Dr or Cr — of each account) is the common practice. Gross Trial Balance (shows both Dr and Cr totals separately) is not common.","Ebook","Other options: Net TB = only balance shown; Gross TB = both Dr and Cr totals shown · Gross TB is not commonly used in practice · Net TB corresponds to Balance Method; Gross TB to Total Method"],
[4,"Expenses and Revenue accounts are NOT balanced in the Ledger — they are only:",["Balanced with previous year figures","Totalled and transferred to Trading A/c or P&L","Carried forward to next year as balance","Posted directly to Balance Sheet"],1,"Expenses and Revenue (Nominal accounts) are only TOTALLED in Ledger — not balanced. Their totals are transferred to Trading A/c or P&L A/c at year-end.","Ebook","Other options: Assets and Liabilities are balanced (they have b/d and c/d balances) · Nominal accounts (expenses, income) are closed at year-end via P&L · Only Real and Personal accounts carry balances to next year"],
[4,"When a ledger account has excess Debit over Credit, what is written on the credit side to balance it?",["'To Balance b/d'","'By Balance c/d'","'To Balance c/d'","'By Balance b/d'"],1,"'By Balance c/d' is written on the credit side when Debit exceeds Credit. This balance is then brought down as 'To Balance b/d' on the debit side of next period.","Ebook","Other options: Excess Dr → write 'By Balance c/d' on Cr side to balance · Excess Cr → write 'To Balance c/d' on Dr side to balance · c/d = carried down; b/d = brought down (next period)"],
[4,"A Personal Account showing a DEBIT balance in the Trial Balance means:",["The person owes us money — Sundry Debtor","We owe money to the person — Sundry Creditor","The account is an expense","The person has paid more than owed"],0,"Personal A/c with Debit balance = Debtor (Sundry Debtors) — the person owes money TO the business. Credit balance = Creditor (Sundry Creditors) — business owes to them.","Ebook","Other options: Dr balance personal = Debtor (they owe us) · Cr balance personal = Creditor (we owe them) · Debtors appear on Balance Sheet Assets side; Creditors on Liabilities side"],
[4,"Which stock figure appears in the Trial Balance?",["Closing Stock","Average Stock","Opening Stock","Both Opening and Closing Stock"],2,"Trial Balance shows OPENING Stock (from previous year's closing balance). Closing Stock is an adjustment item that appears OUTSIDE the Trial Balance.","Ebook","Other options: Opening Stock → Trial Balance Debit side · Closing Stock → NOT in Trial Balance; it is an adjustment · This difference between TB and BS regarding stock is a common MCQ"],
[4,"Which format of Trial Balance is also known as the T / Horizontal Format?",["Journal Format","Vertical Format","Ledger Format","Adjusted Format"],2,"Ledger Format = T / Horizontal Format: two sides (Left = Dr balances, Right = Cr balances), 3 columns each. Journal Format = Vertical Format with 4 columns.","Ebook","Other options: Two formats: Journal (Vertical, 4 columns) and Ledger (Horizontal/T, 2 sides) · Journal format is the more common vertical presentation · Ledger format mirrors the T-account structure"],
[4,"The Trial Balance does NOT show which of the following?",["List of all ledger account balances","Arithmetical accuracy of posted entries","Business profit or loss for the period","Summary of all financial transactions"],2,"Trial Balance does NOT show profit/loss or financial position — that is the job of the P&L Account and Balance Sheet. TB only checks arithmetical accuracy.","Ebook","Other options: TB is a testing tool — not a reporting tool for profit/loss · Profit/Loss → P&L Account; Financial Position → Balance Sheet · TB gives only condensed (summary) information per account"],
[4,"In the Trial Balance, the headings are Debit and Credit. In the Balance Sheet, the headings are:",["Income and Expense","Dr and Cr","Assets and Liabilities","Receipts and Payments"],2,"Balance Sheet headings: Assets (right side) and Liabilities + Capital (left side). Trial Balance headings: Debit and Credit.","Ebook","Other options: TB headings: Debit | Credit · Balance Sheet headings: Liabilities + Capital | Assets · Balance Sheet items: Personal + Real accounts only (Nominal accounts are closed)"],
[4,"What is the key difference between the Total Method and Balance Method of preparing Trial Balance?",["Total Method uses closing balances; Balance Method uses totals","Total Method uses both Dr and Cr totals without balancing; Balance Method uses net closing balances","They are identical — just different names","Balance Method is used only for gross trial balance"],1,"Total Method: accounts NOT balanced — only totalled (Grand Dr total = Grand Cr total). Balance Method: uses closing balances of each account (more common in practice).","Ebook","Other options: Total Method → accounts NOT balanced, Grand totals shown · Balance Method → closing balances only, more common · Both methods result in Dr total = Cr total (if correct)"],
[4,"Real Accounts always show which type of balance in the Trial Balance?",["Credit balance","Debit balance","Either Debit or Credit","Zero balance after balancing"],1,"Real Accounts (assets) always show Debit balance in the Trial Balance. Assets have positive debit balance — what you own.","Ebook","Other options: Real Accounts = asset accounts → always Debit balance · Liability accounts (Personal) → always Credit balance · Nominal accounts are not balanced — they are only totalled"],
[4,"Which of the following advantages does the Trial Balance offer?",["Reveals profit/loss for the year","Shows the financial position of the business","Confirms each ledger balance has been computed correctly","Guarantees complete accuracy of all accounting records"],2,"One key advantage: TB confirms each ledger account balance has been computed correctly and transferred to the right (Dr/Cr) side.","Ebook","Other options: TB does NOT reveal profit/loss — that's P&L's job · TB does NOT show financial position — that's Balance Sheet's job · TB does NOT guarantee complete accuracy — errors of principle/omission are undetected"],
[4,"Two errors cancel each other out — one understates and another overstates by the same amount. This is called:",["Error of Omission","Error of Principle","Compensating Error","Error of Commission"],2,"Compensating Errors: two opposite errors that cancel each other out, so the Trial Balance still agrees. These are not detected by TB.","Ebook","Other options: Compensating errors = two wrongs that make the TB agree (misleadingly) · Error of principle = wrong account TYPE used (e.g., capital item in revenue account) · Error of commission = right amount, wrong account (within same category)"],
[4,"Which accounts appear in the Balance Sheet but NOT in the Trial Balance?",["Opening Stock","Closing Stock","Sundry Debtors","Capital Account"],1,"Closing Stock is an adjustment item — it does NOT appear in the Trial Balance but appears in the Balance Sheet (and Trading Account credit side).","Ebook","Other options: Closing Stock → NOT in TB (it's an adjustment from outside) · Opening Stock → IS in TB (Debit side, brought from previous year) · Sundry Debtors and Capital do appear in TB"],
[5,"Final Accounts consist of three components. What is the CORRECT sequence?",["Balance Sheet → Trading A/c → P&L A/c","P&L A/c → Trading A/c → Balance Sheet","Trading A/c → P&L A/c → Balance Sheet","Journal → Trial Balance → Balance Sheet"],2,"Sequence: Trading Account (Gross Profit/Loss) → Profit & Loss Account (Net Profit/Loss) → Balance Sheet (Financial Position). This is the correct flow.","Ebook","Other options: Trading A/c comes FIRST — it feeds Gross Profit to P&L · P&L feeds Net Profit to Capital Account, which feeds to Balance Sheet · Balance Sheet is LAST — it shows position after all accounts are closed"],
[5,"The Trading Account is prepared to find:",["Net Profit or Net Loss","Gross Profit or Gross Loss","Financial position on a specific date","Total indirect expenses for the year"],1,"Trading Account reveals Gross Profit or Gross Loss — the difference between Selling Price and Cost Price of goods bought and sold.","Ebook","Other options: Gross Profit = from Trading A/c (direct activities only) · Net Profit = from P&L A/c (after indirect expenses) · Financial position = Balance Sheet (not a P&L account)"],
[5,"Which of the following appears on the DEBIT side of the Trading Account?",["Sales","Closing Stock","Opening Stock","Sales Returns credited back"],2,"Trading A/c Debit side: Opening Stock, Purchases, Direct Expenses (wages, carriage inwards, etc.). Credit side: Sales, Sales Returns deducted, Closing Stock.","Ebook","Other options: Opening Stock = Debit side of Trading A/c · Closing Stock = Credit side of Trading A/c (and Balance Sheet) · Sales is on Credit side; Purchases is on Debit side"],
[5,"Closing Stock appears OUTSIDE the Trial Balance. Where does it appear in Final Accounts?",["Debit side of Trading A/c only","Credit side of Trading A/c AND as an asset in Balance Sheet","Debit side of P&L A/c only","Liabilities side of Balance Sheet"],1,"Closing Stock (adjustment item) appears in TWO places: Credit side of Trading A/c (as income/stock remaining) AND as Current Asset in Balance Sheet.","Ebook","Other options: Adjustment Golden Rule: adjustment items treated at TWO places · Closing Stock is NOT in Trial Balance — it's an adjustment from stocktaking · Opening Stock IS in Trial Balance (Debit side)"],
[5,"If the Credit side of Trading Account exceeds the Debit side, the result is:",["Gross Loss","Net Loss","Gross Profit","Net Profit"],2,"Trading A/c Credit > Debit = Gross Profit (Sales > Cost). This is transferred to the Credit side of P&L Account.","Ebook","Other options: Credit > Debit = Profit (more income than cost) in Trading A/c · Debit > Credit = Gross Loss → transferred to DEBIT side of P&L · Same logic: in P&L, Credit > Debit = Net Profit; Debit > Credit = Net Loss"],
[5,"'Carriage Inwards' is classified as which type of expense?",["Indirect Expense — goes to P&L Account","Financial Expense — goes to P&L Account","Direct Expense — goes to Trading Account","Selling Expense — goes to P&L Account"],2,"Carriage Inwards = cost of bringing goods IN (direct to trading activity) → Debit side of Trading Account. Carriage Outwards = indirect (selling) → P&L Account.","Ebook","Other options: Carriage INWARDS → Trading A/c (direct, related to purchasing goods) · Carriage OUTWARDS → P&L A/c (indirect, related to selling goods) · Inward = to bring goods IN = direct cost; Outward = to send goods OUT = selling cost"],
[5,"The Profit & Loss Account starts with which figure from the Trading Account?",["Opening Stock","Net Sales","Gross Profit or Gross Loss","Total Direct Expenses"],2,"P&L Account starts with Gross Profit (credited) or Gross Loss (debited) transferred from Trading Account. P&L then accounts for indirect expenses.","Ebook","Other options: P&L begins where Trading A/c ends — Gross Profit/Loss is the link · P&L does NOT include direct expenses — those are in Trading A/c · P&L records indirect expenses in 4 groups"],
[5,"How many groups of Indirect Expenses are recorded on the Debit side of the P&L Account?",["2","3","4","5"],2,"4 groups of Indirect Expenses: (1) Selling Expenses, (2) Office Expenses, (3) Maintenance Expenses, (4) Financial Expenses.","Ebook","Other options: 4 groups = Selling | Office | Maintenance | Financial · Depreciation falls under Maintenance Expenses · Interest on loan and Discount Allowed = Financial Expenses"],
[5,"Depreciation is classified under which group of Indirect Expenses in the P&L Account?",["Selling Expenses","Office Expenses","Financial Expenses","Maintenance Expenses"],3,"Depreciation falls under Maintenance Expenses (also includes Repairs & Renewals). It goes to the Debit side of P&L Account.","Ebook","Other options: Depreciation → Maintenance Expenses → P&L Debit · Advertising, Carriage Outwards → Selling Expenses · Office Salaries, Rent, Postage → Office Expenses"],
[5,"When Net Profit is determined in the P&L Account, it is transferred to:",["Balance Sheet directly as a separate line","Debit side of Trading A/c","Capital Account (increases capital)","Purchases Account"],2,"Net Profit is transferred to Capital Account, increasing owner's equity. Net Loss reduces Capital. Capital then reflects in the Balance Sheet.","Ebook","Other options: Net Profit → increases Capital A/c (Credit to Capital) · Net Loss → decreases Capital A/c (Debit to Capital) · Capital A/c then appears on Liabilities side of Balance Sheet"],
[5,"The Balance Sheet is defined as a statement that sets out Assets and Liabilities to ascertain financial position. It is prepared:",["For a period (like an income statement)","On a specific date (point-in-time statement)","Monthly with revenue and expense detail","Only at the time of business liquidation"],1,"Balance Sheet is prepared on a SPECIFIC DATE — it shows financial position at a point in time. Unlike P&L which covers a period, Balance Sheet is a snapshot.","Ebook","Other options: Balance Sheet = specific date (snapshot); P&L = period (income/expenses over time) · Balance Sheet is a STATEMENT — not an account · Prepared annually at year-end (or whenever required)"],
[5,"The Balance Sheet is a 'statement' — NOT an 'account'. What is on the LEFT side of the Balance Sheet?",["Assets","Expenses and Losses","Liabilities and Capital","Income and Gains"],2,"Balance Sheet: Left side = Liabilities + Capital; Right side = Assets. Both sides must always be EQUAL (Balance Sheet equation).","Ebook","Other options: Left = Liabilities + Capital (sources of funds) · Right = Assets (uses of funds) · Note: In some formats (UK style), assets are on left — but standard Indian format has Liabilities on left"],
[5,"Which of the following Balance Sheet equations is INCORRECT?",["Assets = Liabilities + Capital","Capital = Assets − Liabilities","Liabilities = Assets − Capital","Capital = Liabilities + Assets"],3,"Capital = Liabilities + Assets is INCORRECT. The correct equation is: Assets = Liabilities + Capital. So Capital = Assets − Liabilities.","Ebook","Other options: A = L + C is the fundamental equation · Capital = A − L (what's left for owner after paying all liabilities) · Liabilities = A − C (what the business owes to outsiders)"],
[5,"Which accounts are shown in the Balance Sheet (NOT all ledger accounts)?",["All nominal accounts only","Personal and Real accounts only","All ledger accounts including nominal","Only capital and liabilities accounts"],1,"Balance Sheet shows only Personal and Real accounts. Nominal accounts (expenses, income) are closed through Trading A/c and P&L A/c before Balance Sheet is prepared.","Ebook","Other options: Nominal accounts are closed to Trading/P&L — they don't appear in Balance Sheet · Trial Balance shows ALL ledger accounts; Balance Sheet shows only Personal + Real · This is a key TB vs BS difference"],
[5,"The Golden Rule for adjustments in Final Accounts states that adjustment items are treated at:",["One place only — either in Trading A/c or Balance Sheet","Three places — Trading A/c, P&L, and Balance Sheet","Two places — both in Profit/Loss statement AND Balance Sheet","No place — adjustments are ignored in final accounts"],2,"Adjustment Golden Rule: Items from Trial Balance → entered at ONE place. Items from Adjustments (outside TB) → treated at TWO places (P&L + Balance Sheet).","Ebook","Other options: TB items → one place only · Adjustment items (outside TB) → always two places · Example: Outstanding expenses → P&L Debit + Balance Sheet Liability"],
[5,"Outstanding Expenses are expenses incurred but NOT yet paid. How are they treated in Final Accounts?",["Added to expenses in P&L A/c only","Shown only on Balance Sheet as a liability","Added to expenses in P&L A/c AND shown as a liability in Balance Sheet","Deducted from expenses in P&L A/c"],2,"Outstanding Expenses: (1) Added to the relevant expense in P&L A/c (Debit side), AND (2) Shown as a liability in Balance Sheet. Two-place adjustment rule.","Ebook","Other options: Outstanding = incurred but not paid = still a cost of the period (P&L) + a debt (BS) · Prepaid expenses = paid but not yet due = deduct from expense (P&L) + shown as asset (BS) · Both are adjustment items → two-place treatment"],
[5,"'Octroi Duty' is an example of which type of expense in Final Accounts?",["Indirect Expense — P&L Account","Financial Expense — P&L Account","Selling Expense — P&L Account","Direct Expense — Trading Account"],3,"Octroi Duty = duty charged for bringing goods into a municipal area. It is a Direct Expense → Debit side of Trading Account.","Ebook","Other options: Direct expenses relate to purchasing/making goods → Trading A/c · Octroi, customs duty, dock dues, import duty = all Direct Expenses · Selling-related expenses like advertising → Indirect (P&L)"],
[5,"An objective of the Trading Account is to help determine:",["Net profit after all expenses","Gross Profit ratio to Sales — for pricing decisions","The financial position of the business","The total indirect expenses for the year"],1,"Trading A/c helps determine Gross Profit ratio to Sales — useful for pricing decisions and identifying direct expense inefficiencies year-over-year.","Ebook","Other options: Gross Profit/Sales ratio → pricing and competitiveness assessment · Net Profit → P&L Account shows this (after all expenses) · Financial position → Balance Sheet shows this"],
[5,"In Final Accounts, 'Interest on Loan' is classified as:",["Direct Expense — Trading Account","Selling Expense — P&L Account","Financial Expense — P&L Account","Maintenance Expense — P&L Account"],2,"Interest on Loan is a Financial Expense → Debit side of P&L Account. Financial Expenses group includes: Interest on loan + Discount Allowed.","Ebook","Other options: 4 groups of indirect expenses: Selling | Office | Maintenance | Financial · Financial expenses = Interest on loan + Discount allowed · Do not confuse with Discount Received (income, Credit side of P&L)"],
[5,"Final Accounts are prepared on the basis of:",["Cash Book directly","Trial Balance","Journal entries only","Ledger accounts without Trial Balance"],1,"Final Accounts (Trading A/c, P&L, Balance Sheet) are prepared from the Trial Balance — plus any adjustments outside the TB.","Ebook","Other options: TB is the basis for Final Accounts — not Journal or Ledger directly · Adjustments (outside TB) are also incorporated · Sequence: Journal → Ledger → TB → Final Accounts"],
[5,"Which of the following is an objective of preparing the Balance Sheet?",["To determine Gross Profit for pricing decisions","To check arithmetical accuracy of ledger accounts","To verify if the firm has sufficient assets to repay liabilities","To record indirect expenses for the year"],2,"One objective of Balance Sheet: to show if the firm has sufficient assets to repay its liabilities (solvency check). Other objectives: show financial position, verify accuracy of final accounts.","Ebook","Other options: Gross Profit objective → Trading Account · Arithmetical accuracy → Trial Balance · Balance Sheet shows solvency, position, net worth — not profit calculation"],
[5,"Which of the following statements about the Balance Sheet is CORRECT?",["It is an account — both sides must balance","It shows performance over a period of one year","It is a statement prepared on a specific date showing assets and liabilities","It records all nominal accounts including expenses"],2,"Balance Sheet is a STATEMENT (not an account), prepared on a SPECIFIC DATE, showing Assets and Liabilities/Capital. It must balance (Assets = Liabilities + Capital) but is still called a statement.","Ebook","Other options: Balance Sheet = Statement (not account); prepared on a DATE (not a period) · P&L Account covers a PERIOD; Balance Sheet covers a DATE · Nominal accounts (expenses, income) are NOT in Balance Sheet — they're in P&L"]
  ]
};
